#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078258 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587" :question/problem "Consider the equation $y''-3y'-4y = 0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y = e^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then what is $\\lambda?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-4{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 -  3\\lambda - 4 = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring, we get\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4) (\\lambda +1) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 4$ and $\\lambda =  -1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078259 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "2065790f-428d-5477-ac77-0c5d07853143" :question/math-academy-id "e-6588" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + \\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} - 2y =0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = 1$ and $\\lambda = -2.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078260 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589" :question/problem "Given that $y = y(x)$ satisfies the equation $y''+7y'+6y = 0,$ which of the following could **not** be $y(x)?$\n\n- $-3e^{-6x}$\n\n- $2e^{-6x}-4e^{-x}$\n\n- $e^{-6x}+2e^{x}$" :question/worked-solution "This is a second-order homogeneous ODE. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=e^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots of the characteristic equation are $\\lambda = -6$ and $\\lambda = - 1.$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6x} + Be^{- x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=-3$ and $B=0.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=2$ and $B=-1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^x,$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346101 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe31ce-c923-562f-b7b6-2d3d698a875e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346103} {:answer/id #uuid "6518dc53-ddad-5ce4-b6e6-bdf04d67e24c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346104} {:answer/id #uuid "3f97654d-23d5-59f2-bb05-4609daf7f8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346105} {:answer/id #uuid "9529f504-7dd7-5213-b1ed-5528511e8cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346106} {:answer/id #uuid "a762a1f9-f24a-56aa-b58a-325678b55ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9529f504-7dd7-5213-b1ed-5528511e8cd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-1$ or $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346106} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6040974-ff1c-500d-9a34-66eb2519d2a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "724b6c69-206a-5ea4-83bf-7187a1c59529" :question/math-academy-id "q-129836" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-{y}^{′}-2y=0$ where $y=y(x).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-1$ or $\\lambda =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "88370a28-bf5c-513d-aec1-6d976995c1bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346110} {:answer/id #uuid "01eab8b3-c7d0-545f-ba47-6cd7069890d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346111} {:answer/id #uuid "c5558a64-a793-5309-9748-ca339be72344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346112} {:answer/id #uuid "409d5a08-2768-5997-a884-4725a7abc6fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346113} {:answer/id #uuid "cc1498fd-9670-5c71-b89c-0afee33afc14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01eab8b3-c7d0-545f-ba47-6cd7069890d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346111} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f8532be-437d-5493-9833-0fba12a1d94e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a00e000f-0aed-57ad-bae8-b7c74a96c1be" :question/math-academy-id "q-130114" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-2{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346115 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48c591be-bc14-50bb-ad87-4399f05b4b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346117} {:answer/id #uuid "13de9d83-c3c7-5e88-ad7e-3f9f8b30352b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346118} {:answer/id #uuid "2227a054-cad8-5720-92f4-b66e871e4f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346119} {:answer/id #uuid "63c3ce1c-245b-5993-a15d-c217fc5b4f86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-3x}+3{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346120} {:answer/id #uuid "cf596fe6-1b5d-5d0e-9a8c-aafefa0af652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2227a054-cad8-5720-92f4-b66e871e4f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346119} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ad6c93-0fe9-5aee-ac8b-fee5245535bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "82e16a64-d25e-5283-bcae-ba7d724711ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-130123" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}+3y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +3 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-1.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{3x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346122 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89f2c543-1b79-5e92-98b9-108a3cdd34bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346124} {:answer/id #uuid "87ec3935-ebf1-5e5e-aa14-4fade6f9ecfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346125} {:answer/id #uuid "859db23e-9b05-5df1-9b15-a83e530ad24b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346126} {:answer/id #uuid "337326d2-c866-57f6-80c8-46cda2cab705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346127} {:answer/id #uuid "4f1bc561-0b11-5903-88c2-eb0998653a50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346128}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "337326d2-c866-57f6-80c8-46cda2cab705" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346127} :answer-field/id #uuid "4dad484e-42b3-5fc2-a470-4b352c2c5f6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15918ac6-5cd0-591e-80ce-75f64c36b95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-130124" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-2.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{-3x}+{e}^{2x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346129 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "538f47cb-7543-55f7-bd05-85f882fb91e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ or $-5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346131} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5b97c4-7353-5b92-b15b-2da4e019a634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346132} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9e7049-7c99-5814-a726-0314b7dc226c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346133} {:answer/id #uuid "bf96bbf5-c9ed-5ccd-9763-fb20d0def9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346134} {:answer/id #uuid "c0310609-6a37-548b-8c14-fcf733f23d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a5b97c4-7353-5b92-b15b-2da4e019a634" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346132} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d1935e-7685-5d1c-a227-e36622433273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a341ef36-052b-58a5-8e5a-622fd4d5ea6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64701" :question/problem "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-2{y}^{′}-15y=0$ where $y=y(t).$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ is a solution, then $\\lambda =$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}-15{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346136 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ef8ba14-809f-5819-868d-938bc628c8ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{6x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346138} {:answer/id #uuid "949de324-fa3b-5141-a7a8-ab80430ef4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346139} {:answer/id #uuid "deb16d9f-0802-5aaf-b3a0-2c256d684198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346140} {:answer/id #uuid "bf89a5b1-f5b7-58f7-b7bd-d8f74d406e10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346141} {:answer/id #uuid "6c141035-9a1f-5906-9df7-355bf3167885" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deb16d9f-0802-5aaf-b3a0-2c256d684198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346140} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce92365-91a0-57e4-8037-9cf3ffc72f08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "006a1cf5-f229-5d79-b13a-133f2907d059" :question/math-academy-id "q-7782" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -6.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -2)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =2.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{2x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23707e6b-3a6e-5d36-85ae-eab04f6ea29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346145} {:answer/id #uuid "f2aee4cd-1134-52ba-9ce5-83e2ce9788d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346146} {:answer/id #uuid "595d0e35-3ee2-581f-ae00-dbd47e89aa42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346147} {:answer/id #uuid "e1d7ea77-e94b-5c00-b6a7-46268ed6bf3e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346148} {:answer/id #uuid "5c8959b8-f72f-54e4-9a79-032d3c251e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346149}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c8959b8-f72f-54e4-9a79-032d3c251e5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346149} :answer-field/id #uuid "18062c8b-a2d0-58c3-aefb-7a1e9ebc66e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2d54f74d-caf3-55e4-a31b-14a288a208d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7783" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}-3{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda -3=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda -1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =1.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d53833c-5d1a-5b1a-83fb-a839e44aaeed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346152} {:answer/id #uuid "0c553daf-ed1c-5061-8cd8-fd2f04f2eede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-3$ or $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346153} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa724e5-7d2a-57e6-aa94-ecdc5e5ca610" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-6$ or $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346154} {:answer/id #uuid "dbff2b53-56c5-52d4-84d3-c10292159c49" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-8$ or $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346155} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa77c62-26e6-5d79-bd6c-be0c2a68991d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2$ or $12$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d53833c-5d1a-5b1a-83fb-a839e44aaeed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4$ or $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346152} :answer-field/id #uuid "76b6779f-1dad-5cee-8d82-5bd27eff7d44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref