lambda =-7$ or $\\lambda =2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346171 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6af9cb84-5dfc-53f4-a68e-aa9f1418c31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346173} {:answer/id #uuid "c916bdad-f4e0-597a-9163-c4d5a1da774a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-5x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346174} {:answer/id #uuid "9304248d-892a-56d8-ae2f-7ce9a364ced3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346175} {:answer/id #uuid "4112d3c6-0842-5590-ae54-232fea3508d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346176} {:answer/id #uuid "53adbd06-f2d2-5857-aba0-615b00520225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53adbd06-f2d2-5857-aba0-615b00520225" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346177} :answer-field/id #uuid "444f8d71-2293-530f-9139-7a05f31812ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "980eb33b-6827-5343-b7cc-71c35035810a" :question/math-academy-id "q-90719" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+15y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +15=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda -5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =3$ or $\\lambda =5.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+B{e}^{5x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be51fa41-33eb-5d1b-968f-d9c371745922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346180} {:answer/id #uuid "01890dea-142c-5b0b-aead-4e5708c6103a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-6x}+B{e}^{-x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346181} {:answer/id #uuid "4ce55ea1-aa77-5e98-b13d-7c3606a4398a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346182} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c25f01-f1ec-52ab-92b0-2b927d8ac063" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346183} {:answer/id #uuid "f797588a-8215-5068-a0e1-5d4cac2a12d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{2x}+B{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ce55ea1-aa77-5e98-b13d-7c3606a4398a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346182} :answer-field/id #uuid "77887c25-0603-5d81-9900-5ac6d5c34d6d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346179}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21d26124-87b6-59dc-b996-f57deec2c2f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-90720" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-7\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y=0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-7\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =1$ or $\\lambda =6.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{6x}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346185 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b918f7b-e6a6-50d9-8354-159909db52a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346187} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7bf6d9-7205-5cdc-81b4-681552c14e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346188} {:answer/id #uuid "31f1a63d-e28b-5304-9340-f3435e56d763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{-5x}-{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346189} {:answer/id #uuid "d37846a4-71bd-53c2-90d5-8137e9611293" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346190} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf71fbd-65e7-5ff0-bb84-f036fd3a58cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{-5x}+3{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7bf6d9-7205-5cdc-81b4-681552c14e60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346188} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d7a282-b22f-5ae1-b068-37406a823265" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ec0270d1-70c2-5347-87eb-345d880dbd64" :question/math-academy-id "q-90725" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+15y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+8\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+15{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +15 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +5)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =-3$ or $\\lambda =-5.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{-5x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only ${e}^{5x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43dbbbd4-2141-5bcc-ba7e-677be6a66521" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346194} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b7e803-9822-5f3b-af3a-8eca526b74f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2(x+4)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346195} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d2bc3e-256c-5a5d-93f7-35cc666ec4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346196} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2a3de0-4dbb-50e2-b9c0-8724058a496c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346197} {:answer/id #uuid "6861242e-8eff-5799-94f3-5b06b6e2fe18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{e}^{2x}+{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6d2bc3e-256c-5a5d-93f7-35cc666ec4b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346196} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfca3dd7-0a4e-541c-b655-a029f427f169" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39e0b422-7623-5ade-9808-a30e9685f26f" :question/math-academy-id "q-90727" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-5\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+6{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =2$ or $\\lambda =3.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{2x}$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $2{e}^{x}$ is not a solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186346199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c8d0525-63b3-54c0-8595-155219dd5452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{6x}+2{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346201} {:answer/id #uuid "1e08ddab-a8ef-5ed6-8eb7-0a4e3a69da41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346202} {:answer/id #uuid "139da781-3c27-50b1-beb8-e122388aa95c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346203} {:answer/id #uuid "32a3ae97-a078-5726-bdd3-13511b5ca4ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{6x}-8{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346204} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e253ac-d8c8-5a5f-a71c-27c5fc4ae744" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e08ddab-a8ef-5ed6-8eb7-0a4e3a69da41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4-{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346202} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebf0763f-310f-5bdd-b956-d9be44e8e438" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a534636-6d6e-563f-921f-29f719aa122b" :question/math-academy-id "q-90729" :question/problem "Given that $y=y(x)$ satisfies the equation ${y}^{″}-10{y}^{′}+24y=0,$ which of the following **cannot** be $y(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-10\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+24{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +24 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda -6)=0,\n$$\n\nand so the solutions of the auxiliary equation  are $\\lambda =4$ or $\\lambda =6.$\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{4x}$ and ${e}^{6x}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $4-{e}^{6x}$ is not a solution."}]}
